### What is target typing in lambda expressions?

Target typing means the compiler determines the lambda's functional interface type from the context in which it appears. The lambda itself has no type.

### How do the four target contexts work?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.function.Supplier;
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;

public class TargetTypingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Context 1: variable assignment
        Supplier<String> supplier = () -> "RELIANCE";
        Callable<String> callable = () -> "RELIANCE";
        // Same lambda, different types!

        System.out.println("Supplier: " + supplier.get());
        System.out.println("Callable: " + callable.call());
    }

    // Context 2: method parameter
    static void processSupplier(Supplier<String> s) {
        System.out.println("Supplier: " + s.get());
    }
    static void processCallable(Callable<String> c) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("Callable: " + c.call());
    }

    // Context 3: return type
    static Supplier<String> createSupplier() {
        return () -> "RELIANCE"; // inferred as Supplier
    }
}
```

```text
Supplier: RELIANCE
Callable: RELIANCE
```

### Why does overloading cause ambiguity?

When two overloaded methods accept different functional interfaces with the same lambda-compatible signature, the compiler cannot choose.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.function.Supplier;
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;

public class AmbiguityDemo {
    // Two overloads with compatible lambda signatures
    static void process(Supplier<String> s) {
        System.out.println("Supplier: " + s.get());
    }
    static void process(Callable<String> c) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("Callable: " + c.call());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // WRONG: compile error! "reference to process is ambiguous"
        // process(() -> "RELIANCE");

        // CORRECT: explicit cast resolves ambiguity
        process((Supplier<String>) () -> "RELIANCE");
        process((Callable<String>) () -> "RELIANCE");

        // CORRECT: typed variable
        Supplier<String> sup = () -> "RELIANCE";
        process(sup);
    }
}
```

```text
Supplier: RELIANCE
Callable: RELIANCE
Supplier: RELIANCE
```

### How is Supplier different from Callable?

| Aspect | Supplier<T> | Callable<T> |
|--------|-------------|-------------|
| Method | `T get()` | `T call() throws Exception` |
| Checked exception | No | Yes (throws Exception) |
| Package | java.util.function | java.util.concurrent |
| Use case | Lazy value, factory | Task that may throw |

A lambda `() -> "x"` matches both because it throws no checked exception. A lambda `() -> { throw new IOException(); }` matches only Callable because Supplier does not allow checked exceptions.

### What are the rules and constraints?

The compiler uses the target type to validate the lambda body. If the target is `Predicate<String>`, the lambda must take one String and return boolean. If the target is `Function<String, Integer>`, the lambda must take one String and return Integer. Mismatched shapes produce compile errors.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TargetTypingMistakes {
    // WRONG: avoid overloading with same-shape FIs
    // static void execute(Supplier<String> s) { }
    // static void execute(Callable<String> c) { }

    // CORRECT: use different method names
    static void executeSync(java.util.function.Supplier<String> s) {
        System.out.println(s.get());
    }
    static void executeAsync(java.util.concurrent.Callable<String> c) throws Exception {
        System.out.println(c.call());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        executeSync(() -> "RELIANCE");  // no ambiguity
        executeAsync(() -> "RELIANCE"); // no ambiguity
    }
}
```

### When should you use explicit casts?

Use explicit casts only when you must work with existing overloaded APIs that you cannot change. For new APIs, use different method names to eliminate ambiguity entirely.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Target typing extends beyond lambdas to method references and conditional expressions. The ternary operator `condition ? supplier : callable` also requires target typing resolution. Understanding target typing helps debug cryptic compiler errors in complex generic method chains.
